WEBVTT

00:03.920 --> 00:04.280
All right.

00:04.320 --> 00:08.480
So now let's understand what can happen if you create clustered index in your table.

00:08.480 --> 00:12.960
So say you created clustered index on the ID column of the customers.

00:12.960 --> 00:18.720
So the first thing that's going to happen is SQL going to physically sort all the data based on the

00:18.720 --> 00:19.600
column ID.

00:19.680 --> 00:25.520
So the rows can rearranged in each data page from the lowest to the highest.

00:25.520 --> 00:32.200
So in the first page we're going to have the first customer ID number one, then 2345 until we reach

00:32.240 --> 00:35.040
in the last page the last customer number 20.

00:35.080 --> 00:41.120
So as you can see the first page has the lowest value and the last page has the highest value.

00:41.120 --> 00:42.560
So that's not all.

00:42.760 --> 00:49.000
The next tip is that SQL is going to go and start structuring and building the B-tree.

00:49.200 --> 00:50.800
So what is a B-tree?

00:50.960 --> 00:53.160
A B-tree is short for balanced tree.

00:53.280 --> 00:58.560
It is hierarchical structure that store the data as a tree upside down.

01:05.540 --> 01:11.940
It starts with the root, the root node, and then it keeps branching out until we reach eventually

01:11.940 --> 01:12.780
the leaves.

01:12.820 --> 01:17.980
Between the leaf nodes and the root node we call this section the intermediate nodes.

01:18.020 --> 01:22.420
So it could be like one level or multiple levels between the root and the leaves.

01:22.500 --> 01:28.300
And once SQL construct the B-tree, it's going to be very easy for SQL to navigate through the B-tree

01:28.300 --> 01:30.940
in order to find specific information.

01:31.020 --> 01:35.260
So let's see how SQL is building the B-tree for the clustered index.

01:35.260 --> 01:40.900
Now, very important to understand that the leaves, the leaf nodes at the B-tree for the clustered

01:40.900 --> 01:45.500
index contain the actual data, the data pages.

01:45.500 --> 01:50.420
So all your nice sorted data pages and your data is stored at the leaf level.

01:50.420 --> 01:54.220
Then after that, SQL can start building the intermediate nodes.

01:54.220 --> 01:58.100
And here the database can use different types of pages.

01:58.100 --> 02:00.020
We have the index page.

02:00.020 --> 02:06.880
So in the index page we cannot find the actual data the entire rose, but instead the index page stores

02:07.160 --> 02:12.520
a key value that contains a pointer to another index page or to a data page.

02:12.600 --> 02:18.280
So, for example, we have here the value one the key, and then the value going to be the ID of the

02:18.280 --> 02:19.080
data page.

02:19.200 --> 02:25.280
So here we don't have like the whole row about the data we have here only a pointer to another data

02:25.320 --> 02:25.800
page.

02:25.920 --> 02:32.680
So here we are telling SQL if you are searching for IDs between 1 and 5 you can locate it at the data

02:32.720 --> 02:35.440
page ID one double point 100.

02:35.480 --> 02:39.880
And then we can store in this index page another pointer where we can tell SQL.

02:39.880 --> 02:45.480
If you are searching between 6 and 10 then you can locate it at the second data page.

02:45.560 --> 02:47.720
So this is the structure of the index page.

02:47.720 --> 02:50.840
It contains only pointers to another page.

02:50.960 --> 02:56.200
And the same thing for the second two pages the SQL can create another index page where it's going to

02:56.240 --> 03:02.680
say if you are searching for IDs between 11 and 15, you can find it at the third page, one double

03:02.720 --> 03:03.880
point 102.

03:04.000 --> 03:06.260
And for the last group between 16 and 20.

03:06.300 --> 03:11.780
We have another pointer to the last page to the page number one double point 103.

03:11.780 --> 03:18.980
So as you can see inside those index pages, we have like a pointer for each group of IDs for each cluster.

03:18.980 --> 03:22.700
So for the group of customers between 1 and 5 we have one pointer.

03:22.860 --> 03:26.380
And for the second group between 6 and 10 we have another pointer.

03:26.380 --> 03:30.380
So that means we don't have here a pointer for each row.

03:30.500 --> 03:33.820
We have a pointer for each group for each cluster.

03:33.980 --> 03:36.740
And that's why we call it clustered index.

03:36.740 --> 03:42.100
And now once SQL is done building the intermediate nodes is going to go and build the last node, the

03:42.100 --> 03:49.660
root node where it says if you are searching for customers between 1 and 10, then go to the index page

03:49.780 --> 03:52.140
with the ID 100.20.

03:52.180 --> 03:58.740
So that means the root node here is pointing to another index page, not directly to the data page.

03:58.740 --> 03:59.420
And the same thing.

03:59.420 --> 04:02.700
We need another pointer for the second index page.

04:02.700 --> 04:09.430
So the customers between 11 and 20 go to the index page with the ID one double point 201.

04:09.430 --> 04:14.870
And this is exactly what can happen if you create a clustered index in SQL.

04:14.990 --> 04:20.110
First it's going to go and physically sort all your data in the databases.

04:20.110 --> 04:26.430
So if it's from the first time sorted randomly, SQL has to arrange everything and sort the data from

04:26.430 --> 04:27.190
the scratch.

04:27.190 --> 04:32.350
And then it's going to go and build this structure where you have in the root node, an index page in

04:32.350 --> 04:35.110
the intermediate nodes, the index pages.

04:35.110 --> 04:40.550
But at the leaf level at the leaves we have the actual data, the data pages.

04:40.550 --> 04:46.470
So now let's see what can happen if you query the table where you search for the ID number 14.

04:46.470 --> 04:48.550
So it's going to check which pointer to use.

04:48.590 --> 04:54.870
Since 14 is in the group between 11 and 20 it's going to go and use the second pointer to the index

04:54.870 --> 04:57.430
page with the ID one double point 201.

04:57.470 --> 05:01.550
And here the SQL going to open this index page and check the pointers.

05:01.550 --> 05:09.050
So since 14 is between 11 and 15 it's going to go and use the pointer to the data page One double point

05:09.090 --> 05:10.210
102.

05:10.250 --> 05:13.890
And with that, SQL located the correct data page.

05:14.010 --> 05:15.010
The third page.

05:15.050 --> 05:20.370
And now SQL can open this data page and find the customer ID number 14.

05:20.370 --> 05:26.210
So as you can see it was very fast for SQL to locate the correct data page with only three jumps from

05:26.210 --> 05:31.570
the root node to the intermediate node, the SQL were able to find fast the correct data page, and

05:31.570 --> 05:38.530
here SQL needs only to read one data page instead of reading as we saw in the heap structure for different

05:38.530 --> 05:39.330
data pages.

05:39.330 --> 05:42.930
And of course you might say, but still here we are reading like three pages.

05:43.250 --> 05:48.930
While reading an index page is very fast compared to the data page, because reading a data page is

05:48.930 --> 05:51.410
always slower than reading an index page.

05:51.410 --> 05:57.650
So as you can see, this B-tree structure, the clustered index structure did help the SQL and the database

05:57.770 --> 06:05.130
to locate the right data in the right data page, without having unnecessary read operations on different

06:05.130 --> 06:06.050
databases.

06:06.050 --> 06:10.610
And this is exactly how the clustered index works in the SQL database.
